# Issue tracker: local markdown

All `.scratch/` paths below resolve against the main worktree root.

Work for this repo is tracked as markdown files under `.scratch/`, one directory per feature.
There is no service and no CLI — every operation below is a file read or a file write, which
is the point: it works in a repo with no remote, and the tickets stay in the main worktree across implementation branches.

Skills never name a backend. They name one of the operations below and read this file to find
out how to perform it here.

## Layout

```
.scratch/
  <feature-slug>/
    01-<slug>.md     # one ticket per file, numbered from 01
    02-<slug>.md
    drafts/          # draft tickets waiting for review; never listed as open
```

`<feature-slug>` is `<intent-number>-<intent-slug>` when the tickets come from an intent doc, for example `0003-to-issues` for `docs/intent/0003-to-issues.md`. A directory name is then enough to find the intent behind a ticket. Tickets written without an intent use a short free slug, as before.

Every ticket file opens with a short header block, then the body:

```markdown
# <title>

Status: open
Blocked by: 01, 03
Made: 2026-09-28
Use by: 2026-10-12

<body>

## Comments
```

`Status:` is one of `open`, `in-progress`, `done`. `Blocked by:` is omitted when there is nothing blocking. `Made:` is the date the ticket was published. `Use by:` is `Made:` plus `use_by_days` from `.magito/config.toml`, or 14 days when that key is absent. Keep all four lines directly under the title so a skill can read a ticket's state without parsing the whole file.

A ticket whose `Use by:` date has passed is **expired**. Before building an expired ticket, re-check it against the current code, and update or close it if the code has moved. Never delete an expired ticket.

## Ticket identifiers

The path, or `<feature-slug>/NN` in prose. Numbers restart at `01` in each feature directory,
so a bare `03` means nothing on its own — always carry the feature slug with it. Reference
the path in commit messages so `reviewing-changes` can find the spec behind a diff.

When given a file path, an absolute path is used directly. A path starting with `.scratch/`
is relative to the main worktree root. A path starting with a feature slug, such as
`0001-hello/01-hello.md`, is relative to that root's `.scratch/` directory.

## List open tickets

Read every `.scratch/*/[0-9][0-9]-*.md` and keep the ones whose `Status:` is not `done`.
This glob does not match files under `drafts/`, so a draft is never listed as open.
Report them as `<feature-slug>/NN — <title>`, grouped by feature. Report `(expired)` after
the title of any open ticket whose `Use by:` date has passed.

## Fetch a ticket

Read the file. The user normally passes the path or the feature and number directly; if only
a number is given and more than one feature directory exists, ask which feature rather than
guessing.

## Find a published ticket

Read all `.scratch/*/[0-9][0-9]-*.md` under the main worktree root, including done tickets.
Match the exact `Publication-ID: <identity>` line in the body. Return all matching paths.
The glob excludes draft tickets. A read error is an uncertain result, never an empty result.

## Publish a ticket

Write a new file at `.scratch/<feature-slug>/<NN>-<slug>.md`, creating the directory if it
does not exist. `NN` is the next unused number in that directory, zero-padded to two digits.
Start it with the header block above, `Status: open`.

Write `Made:` as today's date and `Use by:` as described under the header block.

Before writing the first file under `.scratch/`, make sure `.scratch/` is a line in
`.git/info/exclude`, using the shell form from `setup-magito`'s *Private-state excludes*
section. A line already present is left alone.

## Comment on a ticket

Append to the `## Comments` section at the bottom of the file, newest last, each entry
prefixed with the date.

## Close a ticket

Set `Status: done` in the file. Leave the file in place — the history is the record, and
deleting it strands every ticket that lists it under `Blocked by:`.

## Link a sub-ticket

Not used. The intent doc is the parent. Every ticket links to it in its body, and the
directory name carries the intent number.

## Blocking edges

The `Blocked by:` line in the child ticket, holding the blockers' numbers within the same
feature directory. A ticket is ready to start when every number it lists has `Status: done`.
Cross-feature blockers are written as `<other-feature>/NN`.

To add a blocker, add its number to the child's `Blocked by:` line, separated by a comma. If
the child has no `Blocked by:` line yet, add one directly under `Status:`. Read the line back
afterwards; a number already on it is left alone.

## Pull requests

Tracking tickets in files says nothing about how work lands. A repo can keep its tickets here
and still open a pull request on a remote — `implement` decides that from the remote, not from
this file, and PR creation stays on `gitflow.sh pr` either way (`docs/adr/0015`).

Where there genuinely is no remote, `implement` stops at its own checkpoint instead: it shows
the diff and the review summary and waits for explicit approval before
`bash <skills>/implement/scripts/gitflow.sh merge`. That checkpoint *is* the human's gate.
(`<skills>` is your tool's installed skills directory — `~/.claude/skills` for Claude Code,
`~/.agents/skills` for most others.)
